    def limits(self):
        a, B = self.a, self.B
        a("## 8. Limits of this analysis\n")
        a("- Two lumps with dead time are fitted on 39 + 15 events; the split (C1, K) is resolved only to about +-40 % and C1 can be as low as 4 kJ/K, so every short-term number carries that, and the C/2 case bounds it.\n- The frozen-power study uses the measured fan and pump trajectory as the plan; a planner that chooses the fan would differ. The sink is unknown; the capacity table is an extrapolation in the sink.\n- The short-spike table is simulated, not observed.\n- G below full fan, G with the fan stopped, the pump above 36 C at the floor, and the dead time at the 15 % pump floor are extrapolations or estimates, marked as such.\n- Fan swap to iCUE LINK fans changes G(rpm) and the spool: all fan numbers are for the current nine fans with one tach.\n- The Parquet copy ends 2026-11-05 ~07:00 UTC; four days, 52.6 h with CPU power, no summer sink.")
        a(f"- Ray: `ray status` is broken by a click traceback here and no Ray cluster was running. The bootstrap ({B['draws']} draws) and the step-test Monte-Carlo ({self.ST['cpus']} tasks of 1 CPU, 6 of 24 cores, the machine was already loaded by another job) each ran on one local Ray cluster started by the run and shut down at its end ({B['how']}). The interpreter was the Python of the `other_project` venv (Ray 2.54.1, as `docs/model.md` did) because the conda Python's Ray lacks the dashboard dependencies; a first start with it failed and left its own `gcs_server` (PID 48004), which I stopped, as I did the stale serial bootstrap of the previous worker (PID 35964). Nothing of other projects was started, stopped or touched; the dashboard agents left over from an older, dead Ray session were left alone.\n")
